# Line endings are LF in the repository and LF in the working tree, on every
# platform.
#
# Without this, Git checks files out with CRLF on Windows, and the first CI run
# this project ever had failed because of it: a test scans docs/for-llms.md for
# ```smarsh fences with /```smarsh\n/, which matches nothing against `\r\n`, so
# the test concluded the page had lost its examples. The page was fine. The
# checkout was different.
#
# Normalising here fixes the class rather than the instance. The regexes that
# tripped over it also accept `\r?\n` now, because a file can still arrive with
# CRLF from an editor or a zip, and a test that only passes on a correctly
# configured checkout is a test that will surprise someone.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Nothing here is binary, but say so for the ones where a mangled byte would be
# silent rather than obvious.
*.pem -text
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
